 81SR c  1985Li12: source from {+58}Ni({+28}Si,|ap) at E({+28}Si)=95 MeV,       
 81SR2c  alternating 120-s bombardment and 180-s counting cycles, Ge(Li);       
 81SR3c  measured E|g, I|g(t). Data from 1981Li12 reappraised.                  
 81SR c  1982De36: {+81}Y from {+32}S induced reactions on {+54}Fe, {+58}Ni and 
 81SR2c  {+50}Cr, E=100-160 MeV, |b recoil tof mass spectrometer, plastic scin, 
 81SR3c  Ge(Li), x-ray detector.                                                
 81SR c  1981Li12: heavy ion reactions, measured with Ge(Li), intrinsic         
 81SR2c  germanium, plastic detectors.                                          
 81SR c  The adopted decay scheme is essentially that of 1985Li12 and differs   
 81SR2c  fundamentally from that of 1982De36. It relies heavily on tentative J|p
 81SR3c  assignments to Sr excited states deduced from (|a,xn|g) data.          
 81SR cE           1982De36 report endpoint energy of 4520 {I227} for           
 81SR2cE T{-1/2}=70 s |b{++} from A=81 |b-recoil nuclei. They attribute this to 
 81SR3cE g.s. feeding, but |DE is too big to preclude attribution to excited    
 81SR4cE states up to |?200 keV. It was, presumably, feeding the 203            
 81SR5cE level that was observed.                                               
 81SR cE TI        From transition intensity balance at each level, except where
 81SR2cE otherwise noted.                                                       
 81SR cE TI(B)     Because the 89 level to 79 level transition is unobserved,   
 81SR2cE only |e+|b{++} branching (|?36.5%) to this pair of levels can be       
 81SR3cE deduced. However, if J|p({+81}Y g.s.)=5/2+, most of the feeding would  
 81SR4cE be expected to go to the (7/2+) 89 level. Decay to a level would       
 81SR5cE be allowed (log {Ift}|<5.9) if I(|e+|b{++})|>16.5%, so                 
 81SR6cE an expected I(|e+|b{++}) would be |<16.5% to the 79 level and rest     
 81SR7cE expected to feed the 89 level. In the dataset the upper-limits are     
 81SR8cE listed for these two levels.                                           
